  "response": "## Verdict: request changes — don't block\n\nThe DST finding on its own doesn't justify a block, and the scan's own numbers say why. `crime_00017`:\n\n```\n\"severity\": 0.6,  \"confidence\": 0.8,  \"agent_risk\": 0.3,\n\"blast_radius\": 0,  \"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 0,  \"test_gap\": 0.5\n```\n\nNothing imports it yet — I confirmed by grep, `tomorrowMs` has zero call sites in the fixture, matching `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`. A latent helper with no callers is a \"fix before it grows teeth,\" not a \"stop the line.\" The `effort` is `\"medium\"`, which means the fix is a real change (pulling in a calendar-aware lib), not a one-line nit you demand inline.\n\nWhat I'd put in the review comment, quoting the finding verbatim:\n\n> **DST-Naive Day Math** — `src/date.ts:52`\n> \"1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.\"\n> - `\"+ 86400000 (day) @L52\"`\n> - `\"lines: 52\"`\n> - `\"use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math\"`\n>\n> Fix shape: *\"use a DST-aware library; never add seconds to a wall-clock date.\"*\n\nTwo things escalate this to a block, and neither is true today:\n\n1. **A caller appears that keys on the calendar date** — billing periods, trial expiry, a `YYYY-MM-DD` day key. `test_gap: 0.5` means no test pins the behaviour, so a wrong-day bug ships silently.\n2. **The file's existing timezone debt gets wired in.** `src/date.ts` already carries a *high*-severity `mixed_utc_local_methods` (`crime_00009`: `\"d\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41`) and a medium `timezone_unsafe_parse` (`crime_00011`: `unsafe literals: \"2026-12-25T07:00:00\", \"2026-12-20\"`). Feed a locally-parsed `Date` into `tomorrowMs` and the two bugs compound — a wrong zone *and* a wrong day length.\n\nI'd ask the author to fix it in this PR since they're already in the file, and I'd flag `crime_00009` as the higher-priority one they've now put a neighbour next to.\n\n## The two wrong days per year\n\nThe helper is wrong on **the two DST transition days of whichever timezone the result is interpreted in.** Concretely, for 2026:\n\n| Zone | Wrong dates (2026) | Real day length | What `+86_400_000` does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| US/Canada | **Sun 8 Mar** and **Sun 1 Nov** | 23 h / 25 h | see below |\n| EU/UK | **Sun 29 Mar** and **Sun 25 Oct** | 23 h / 25 h | same |\n| Australia (AEDT) | **Sun 5 Apr** and **Sun 4 Oct** | 25 h / 23 h | reversed order |\n\nThe two failure modes are not symmetric:\n\n- **Spring forward (23-hour day).** From local midnight, adding 24 real hours lands at **01:00 the next day** — an hour late. The calendar date is still right, so date-keyed logic survives; anything that preserves wall-clock time (a 9am reminder, a daily cutoff) drifts an hour.\n- **Fall back (25-hour day).** From local midnight, adding 24 real hours lands at **23:00 the *same* day** — \"tomorrow\" is still today. This is the dangerous one: a day key, a billing boundary, or a `>= tomorrow` expiry check is off by a full day for every user in that zone.\n\nTwo qualifiers worth having in the review, since they're why this is medium and not high:\n\n- It's **zone-dependent, not universal.** In UTC, Arizona, most of Asia, or anywhere without DST, `tomorrowMs` is exactly correct 365 days a year. The bug only exists once the timestamp is rendered or compared in a DST-observing local zone.\n- The tool's `suggested_actions` says the replacement should know \"about DST **and leap years**\" — the leap-year half doesn't apply to this helper. Unix time has no leap seconds, and Feb 28→29 is an ordinary 24-hour day. DST is the entire failure surface here.\n\n## The fix I'd ask for\n\n```ts\nimport { Temporal } from \"temporal-polyfill\";\n\nexport function tomorrow(now: Temporal.ZonedDateTime): Temporal.ZonedDateTime {\n  return now.add({ days: 1 }); // 23h, 24h, or 25h — whatever that calendar day actually is\n}\n```\n\nThe signature change is the point: taking a `ZonedDateTime` instead of a bare `number` forces the caller to name the timezone the \"day\" belongs to, which is the question the current `nowMs: number` signature lets everyone skip. If pulling in Temporal is out of scope for this PR, `date-fns-tz`'s `addDays` with an explicit zone is the smaller version — but a bare `number` in, `number` out cannot be made correct, because the ambiguity is in the type.",
